I now have all the information needed. Here is a complete report on all three issues:

---

## Issue 1: Slash commands bypass allowedUserIds

**File:** `/Users/lab3/Documents/lab3/OpenACP/src/adapters/discord/adapter.ts`, lines 167-186

```typescript
// adapter.ts, lines 167-186
private setupInteractionHandler(): void {
  this.client.on('interactionCreate', async (interaction) => {
    try {
      if (interaction.isChatInputCommand()) {
        await handleSlashCommand(interaction, this)  // <-- no user check here
        return
      }

      if (interaction.isButton()) {
        const handled = await this.permissionHandler.handleButtonInteraction(interaction)
        if (!handled) {
          await setupButtonCallbacks(interaction, this)
        }
      }
    } catch (err) {
      log.error({ err }, '[DiscordAdapter] interactionCreate handler error')
    }
  })
}
```

The slash command is dispatched directly to `handleSlashCommand()` with no authorization check whatsoever. Compare this to **regular messages** in the same adapter — they go through `core.handleMessage()` which calls `securityGuard.checkAccess()`:

**File:** `/Users/lab3/Documents/lab3/OpenACP/src/core/core.ts`, lines 214-228
```typescript
// core.ts, lines 214-228
const access = this.securityGuard.checkAccess(message);
if (!access.allowed) {
  log.warn({ userId: message.userId, reason: access.reason }, "Access denied");
  ...
  return;
}
```

**File:** `/Users/lab3/Documents/lab3/OpenACP/src/core/security-guard.ts`, lines 11-19
```typescript
checkAccess(message: IncomingMessage):
  ...
  if (config.security.allowedUserIds.length > 0) {
    const userId = String(message.userId);
    if (!config.security.allowedUserIds.includes(userId)) {
```

**Telegram comparison:** The Telegram adapter enforces access at the bot middleware level, line 206-209 of `src/adapters/telegram/adapter.ts`:
```typescript
// telegram/adapter.ts, lines 206-209
this.bot.use((ctx, next) => {
  const chatId = ctx.chat?.id ?? ctx.callbackQuery?.message?.chat?.id;
  if (chatId !== this.telegramConfig.chatId) return;  // only the single configured chatId
  return next();
});
```

Telegram's model is chat-scoped (one fixed `chatId`), so only members of that chat can interact. Discord has no equivalent gate — any Discord user in the server can use slash commands, and `allowedUserIds` from `security.allowedUserIds` is never consulted for interactions.

---

## Issue 2: config.ts Discord default botToken

**File:** `/Users/lab3/Documents/lab3/OpenACP/src/core/config.ts`, lines 153-169

```typescript
// config.ts, lines 153-169
const DEFAULT_CONFIG = {
  channels: {
    telegram: {
      enabled: false,
      botToken: "YOUR_BOT_TOKEN_HERE",   // <-- placeholder string
      chatId: 0,
      ...
    },
    discord: {
      enabled: false,
      botToken: "YOUR_DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN_HERE",  // <-- placeholder string
      guildId: "",
      ...
    },
  },
```

Both `telegram.botToken` and `discord.botToken` use placeholder strings in `DEFAULT_CONFIG`. This is consistent in the defaults object.

However, the critical difference is in the **Zod schema**. The top-level `channels` is typed as `z.record(z.string(), BaseChannelSchema)` (line 101), where `BaseChannelSchema` only covers `enabled` and `adapter` — it does not define `botToken` as a typed field with `z.string().min(1)` or similar validation. There is no schema-level guard that would reject `"YOUR_DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN_HERE"` as an invalid value. Both adapters would successfully parse the config with the placeholder and only fail at runtime when the Discord/Telegram API rejects the token.

